1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,140
It's the greatest freight transportation system in the world.

2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,360
A complex network woven into the very fabric of the United States.

3
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:23,420
From the early iron horses to today's sleek steel stallions.

4
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:29,840
They grind up hillsides, sneak through valleys, thunder across prairies.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,840
to deliver the goods.

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:36,460
Now, Great Trains Plus on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:53,680
Welcome to North Platte, Nebraska,

8
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,580
home of the largest rail yard in the world.

9
00:00:56,581 --> 00:01:00,820
Union Pacific's Bailey Yard.

10
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:04,520
The yard is approximately 3,500 acres.

11
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:08,100
It's 8 miles long, about 2 miles wide.

12
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,100
No other rail facility like it in the world.

13
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,240
Rail yards are the hubs of railroad operations.

14
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,480
Here, freight cars from across the system are funneled in,

15
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,580
sorted, and then reassigned to new trains

16
00:01:20,581 --> 00:01:23,220
that will bring them closer to their final destination.

17
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,960
Just about anything that's produced in the United States

18
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:27,320
is shipped by rail.

19
00:01:27,321 --> 00:01:29,600
So if you're talking about wine or cheese,

20
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:33,860
fresh vegetables, televisions, radios, automobiles,

21
00:01:33,861 --> 00:01:36,760
automobile parts, you name it, just about everything

22
00:01:36,761 --> 00:01:42,160
that America uses on a daily basis comes through North Platte.

23
00:01:42,161 --> 00:01:44,440
The Bailey Yard is strategically located

24
00:01:44,441 --> 00:01:46,760
in the center of the Union Pacific Rail Network.

25
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:51,720
The facility is so large, you can't

26
00:01:51,721 --> 00:01:54,460
see more than a third of it from any point in the yard.

27
00:01:55,960 --> 00:02:05,320
Well, that is, except from the computer screens of the command center, where all rail activity

28
00:02:05,321 --> 00:02:07,920
on the yard is monitored.

29
00:02:07,921 --> 00:02:15,460
What we do in the BCC, Bailey Command Center, is coordinating the movement of between 130

30
00:02:15,461 --> 00:02:18,800
and 180 trains per day.

31
00:02:18,801 --> 00:02:24,840
Utilizing computer-based controls, the center monitors all traffic over Bailey Yard's 315

32
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:31,340
miles of track. In addition to locomotive movements, workers process 14,000 freight

33
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:33,463
cars through the yard each day.

34
00:02:33,464 --> 00:02:37,160
For some of these cars, their first stop is one of the

35
00:02:37,161 --> 00:02:40,080
two classification yards, or hump yards.

36
00:02:40,081 --> 00:02:42,707
This is where we process eastbound trains.

37
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:45,460
Trains that come into North Platte are going

38
00:02:45,461 --> 00:02:48,743
to be sorted here to their four destinations.

39
00:02:48,744 --> 00:02:51,880
So we are taking these cars apart that have

40
00:02:51,881 --> 00:02:54,340
come in on a train, and then we're sorting them

41
00:02:54,341 --> 00:02:56,960
to go out destinations further points from here.

42
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,280
The hump is a three-story man-made hill

43
00:03:00,281 --> 00:03:04,200
that allows up to four cars a minute to roll into its bowl.

44
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:07,000
Gravity pulls them off the hill.

45
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:09,580
The computer picks up the weight of the car

46
00:03:09,581 --> 00:03:11,320
as it goes over the scale rails here.

47
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,000
The car then passes through a system

48
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:17,600
of computerized retarders, or brakes,

49
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:21,040
that pinch the wheels as they go through to slow the car down.

50
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:29,180
It slows them down accordingly, not only to distance that it needs to travel to the other

51
00:03:29,181 --> 00:03:34,120
end, but it also computes how much distance is left in the rail.

52
00:03:34,121 --> 00:03:37,600
And the computer will go all the way from 12 mile an hour down to one mile an hour if

53
00:03:37,601 --> 00:03:43,180
necessary to allow for safe and careful handling of the freight that's inside these cars.

54
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:47,600
It's pretty amazing that the amount of tonnage that's going over this hill, how softly it's

55
00:03:47,601 --> 00:03:50,880
handled down here when it hits the bottom end of things.

56
00:03:50,881 --> 00:03:55,880
The destination of each car is entered into a computer.

57
00:03:55,881 --> 00:03:57,880
It automatically switches the track,

58
00:03:57,881 --> 00:04:01,880
so the car rolls directly onto its assigned rail.

59
00:04:01,881 --> 00:04:05,880
At the trim tower at the other end of the bowl are trim crews,

60
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:07,880
and what they do is they actually build the trains

61
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:09,880
that are going to leave North Platte.

62
00:04:09,881 --> 00:04:10,880
So we take them apart here,

63
00:04:10,881 --> 00:04:13,880
they put them back together down there.

64
00:04:13,881 --> 00:04:16,880
Remote-controlled switch engines,

65
00:04:16,881 --> 00:04:20,200
less powerful locomotives used for switching cars in a rail yard,

66
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:25,740
assemble the trains when enough freight cars going to a single location have been humped.

67
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:30,840
The hump used to be the focal point of the railroad yard.

68
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:33,800
Pretty much all traffic was handled in this fashion.

69
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:39,820
What's changed that has taken the pressure off of this are trains like you see leaving over there.

70
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,460
They leave their originating point all in one big chunk.

71
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:45,920
They arrive at their destination in one big chunk.

72
00:04:45,921 --> 00:04:51,360
Unit freight, or an entire train consisting of the same type of freight, like a coal train,

73
00:04:51,361 --> 00:04:55,300
is treated differently, since it doesn't need to be reassigned.

74
00:04:55,301 --> 00:05:00,640
For unit freight, there are 26 run-through tracks, some with pad areas,

75
00:05:00,641 --> 00:05:06,700
which allow trains to pull up, refuel, and depart without having to detach from the locomotive.

76
00:05:06,701 --> 00:05:12,420
Every train that comes in must have a mechanical inspection by our car forces.

77
00:05:12,421 --> 00:05:17,760
We have a NASCAR pit team concept for our locomotives.

78
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:23,300
They're a team of five men and women that attack each locomotive that comes in where they had to be fueled.

79
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:26,100
The oil has to be checked. They shine the windshields.

80
00:05:26,101 --> 00:05:27,580
They get the locomotive ready to go.

81
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:31,740
Our goal is to do it in 45 minutes to service the train completely.

82
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:38,260
And then we expect the train crew to be here as soon as we drop our protection, which is blue flags.

83
00:05:38,261 --> 00:05:45,200
In addition to these pit stops, the Bailey Yard boasts a locomotive repair depot the

84
00:05:45,201 --> 00:05:48,140
size of five football fields.

85
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:50,840
This is not a heavy duty repair facility.

86
00:05:50,841 --> 00:05:57,200
What we do here is quarterly maintenance, semi-annual, annual and tri-annual standard

87
00:05:57,201 --> 00:05:59,980
maintenance on our locomotives.

88
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:06,660
And just as important, rail cars are serviced at the Bailey Yard as well.

89
00:06:06,661 --> 00:06:10,160
Part of the issue we have with Duranmas on occasion

90
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:13,760
are our wheels or axles failing en route.

91
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:15,700
And so our car department employees

92
00:06:15,701 --> 00:06:18,700
really focus in on the conditions of wheels and axles

93
00:06:18,701 --> 00:06:21,780
as they're coming through our run-through facilities.

94
00:06:21,781 --> 00:06:26,140
Four-person crews sort, service, and repair locomotive tires

95
00:06:26,141 --> 00:06:28,140
at the train yard.

96
00:06:28,141 --> 00:06:32,220
The repair facility handles roughly 50 cars daily.

97
00:06:32,221 --> 00:06:35,780
The repair facility handles roughly 50 cars daily,

98
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:40,100
totaling approximately 10,000 pairs of wheels each year.

99
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:43,760
What used to take 12 days to repair

100
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:48,080
takes crews 8 to 12 minutes to get a train back on track.

101
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:52,540
And most important of all are the people at the Bailey Yard.

102
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:57,180
Today, the train yard is home to approximately 2,000 Union Pacific employees

103
00:06:57,181 --> 00:07:00,740
that work around the clock 365 days a year.

104
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:06,440
inspecting over 14,000 freight cars a day and keeping freight moving.

105
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:10,400
There's an absolute production mandate at Norplat,

106
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:15,200
whether it's rain, snow, sun, blizzard, high winds.

107
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:18,500
We must hit those production targets because if we don't,

108
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:20,780
we will back up the railroad on both sides.

109
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:24,820
And we cannot have that kind of impact on the Union Pacific system.

110
00:07:24,821 --> 00:07:35,260
The Bailey Yard may be the largest rail yard servicing the largest railroad in North America,

111
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:42,600
but it's also a mammoth cog in a much larger machine.

112
00:07:42,601 --> 00:07:50,580
We're talking about an $80 billion a year industry.

113
00:07:50,581 --> 00:07:56,260
A system so large, it encompasses over 140,000 miles of track

114
00:07:56,261 --> 00:07:58,060
and extends to nearly every corner

115
00:07:58,061 --> 00:08:00,960
of the continental United States.

116
00:08:00,961 --> 00:08:02,700
And it's more than that.

117
00:08:02,701 --> 00:08:06,900
It's approximately 38,000 locomotives, 1.7 million

118
00:08:06,901 --> 00:08:08,120
freight cars.

119
00:08:08,121 --> 00:08:11,920
Its trains average 100 cars long, extending over a mile

120
00:08:11,921 --> 00:08:13,980
and a half.

121
00:08:13,981 --> 00:08:20,000
It's 1.8 billion tons of freight crossing the country each year.

122
00:08:20,001 --> 00:08:23,760
but it was the movement of freight that was the driving force behind the creation of the railroad.

123
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:34,520
The railroad business traces its roots back to rudimentary horse-drawn carts like these,

124
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:40,180
navigating rails in coal mines and stone quarries in the 1820s.

125
00:08:41,140 --> 00:08:45,920
The whole principle is that you have steel wheels on steel rails,

126
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:48,800
and that lowers the coefficient of friction.

127
00:08:48,801 --> 00:08:57,020
You could pull more with less power on a steel rail than if you were trying to pull a wagon over the mud or dirt roads.

128
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:02,100
Railways that utilized horses to move public goods soon emerged.

129
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:08,660
After 1830, with the Tom Fum and the first successful steam engines, the best friend of Charleston,

130
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:15,920
and those early ones, they proved that steam was reliable and more powerful than horsepower.

131
00:09:15,921 --> 00:09:23,840
steam engines quickly replaced horses and mules on rail operations and it didn't take long for

132
00:09:23,841 --> 00:09:28,560
railroad entrepreneurs to set their sights on capturing the american freight business

133
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,920
a market cornered by the growing east coast canals just about the time it was taking them to build

134
00:09:35,921 --> 00:09:40,160
the canals to a standard where they would be really useful you could build a railroad next

135
00:09:40,161 --> 00:09:45,500
to the canal and just beat the pants off the canal boats with a train.

136
00:09:45,501 --> 00:09:50,840
And the railroad simply technologically replaced the canals almost overnight.

137
00:09:50,841 --> 00:09:56,000
Laborers built tracks as fast as humanly possible.

138
00:09:56,001 --> 00:10:02,920
By the 1850s, railroad tracks extended from the East Coast to the Great Lakes.

139
00:10:02,921 --> 00:10:09,840
In 1869, the Union Pacific and Central Pacific Railroads met at Promontory Summit, Utah,

140
00:10:09,841 --> 00:10:12,340
and completed the Transcontinental Railroad.

141
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:18,080
The railroad business expanded very rapidly

142
00:10:18,081 --> 00:10:20,320
because you could make a lot of money at it.

143
00:10:20,321 --> 00:10:22,760
There was hardly a holler in West Virginia

144
00:10:22,761 --> 00:10:26,060
that didn't have a branch line reaching a coal mine.

145
00:10:26,061 --> 00:10:29,960
There was hardly a hill or valley in the Pacific Northwest

146
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:32,460
that didn't have a lumber railroad in it.

147
00:10:32,461 --> 00:10:35,840
Railroads changed the landscape of America,

148
00:10:35,841 --> 00:10:39,000
creating new towns along their tracks,

149
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:42,600
supplying the new population with building materials and other necessities,

150
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:46,360
and carrying far-flung crops to markets back east.

151
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:51,980
They could send their money and their gold and stuff back from California to the bankers in the east.

152
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:56,580
All of the things that people wanted and needed could be brought in by train.

153
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:01,220
But moving America's freight didn't become the complex system of rails

154
00:11:01,221 --> 00:11:04,040
that stretches to every corner of the nation overnight.

155
00:11:04,041 --> 00:11:13,620
Today, American railroads move more than 5,000 ton miles of freight per person every year,

156
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:18,260
more than any country in the world. But getting there began with tycoons,

157
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:25,400
like Cornelius Vanderbilt, Jay Gould, and Leland Stanford competing to dominate the market.

158
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:30,220
What separates the United States from the rest of the world is that the American railroads

159
00:11:30,221 --> 00:11:32,060
were all private enterprise.

160
00:11:32,061 --> 00:11:35,560
They were all private companies, highly competitive companies,

161
00:11:35,561 --> 00:11:39,260
often run by robber barons who would have financial wars

162
00:11:39,261 --> 00:11:42,060
and the whole business.

163
00:11:42,061 --> 00:11:44,960
Due to the infighting among the rival railroads,

164
00:11:44,961 --> 00:11:48,080
no cohesive system existed to move freight efficiently

165
00:11:48,081 --> 00:11:51,880
across the various independently owned lines.

166
00:11:51,881 --> 00:11:53,880
During the First World War, there was concern

167
00:11:53,881 --> 00:11:55,580
that the railroads wouldn't be able to keep up

168
00:11:55,581 --> 00:11:57,880
with the demand for service,

169
00:11:57,881 --> 00:12:02,020
and the federal government took over the operation of the railroads

170
00:12:02,021 --> 00:12:04,020
under the United States Railway Administration.

171
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:08,360
Their idea was to standardize railroad operating procedures

172
00:12:08,361 --> 00:12:11,020
and try to make the railroads more efficient.

173
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:16,700
Soon after the war, the government returned the rails to private enterprise.

174
00:12:17,340 --> 00:12:19,680
Twenty years later, by World War II,

175
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:24,140
a collection of independent railroads operated as a coordinated system,

176
00:12:24,141 --> 00:12:26,500
and no government intervention was needed.

177
00:12:26,501 --> 00:12:32,440
The steel backbone of the nation played its part in winning the war.

178
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:38,560
But by the 1950s, the railroad's monopoly on freight transportation was quickly eroded.

179
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:45,180
Trucking and the newly created interstate highway system threatened to derail the railroads.

180
00:12:45,540 --> 00:12:51,680
Then the railroads, which were a network of little feudal domains of different railroads,

181
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:56,460
all competing with each other in a system that was set up to compete among each other,

182
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:00,580
were suddenly having to compete with a new outside competitor.

183
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:06,480
Railroads responded by merging, as well as upgrading and modernizing.

184
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:12,820
And today, freight trains are still the number one way to move goods long distances.

185
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:17,220
An early pioneer was General Electric.

186
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:23,280
Since 1907, GE owned and manufactured locomotives.

187
00:13:23,281 --> 00:13:32,820
At this factory in Erie, Pennsylvania, 208-ton, 44,000-horsepower diesel electric beasts roared to life.

188
00:13:32,821 --> 00:13:39,040
General Electric needs approximately 26 days to completely build one of these steel giants,

189
00:13:39,041 --> 00:13:42,940
and the last 12 days are spent in this final assembly building.

190
00:13:42,941 --> 00:13:49,680
This is Station 1, the first station of a four-station final assembly process for the locomotive.

191
00:13:49,681 --> 00:13:55,720
In station one, you can see where the platform is brought in and inverted in the upside-down position.

192
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:02,640
Workers install sensitive electronics and piping to the undercarriage.

193
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:08,360
Next is a staging area for pre-built components waiting to be installed.

194
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:16,020
This is an example of the Evolution Series engine that is really the heart and soul of the Evolution Series locomotive.

195
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:24,460
The 44,000 horsepower diesel engine produces with 12 cylinders what the previous engines needed 16 cylinders to accomplish.

196
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:31,980
The new Evolution Series locomotive engine is 3% more fuel efficient than its predecessor engines.

197
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:37,620
The average locomotive will burn approximately 350,000 gallons a year.

198
00:14:37,621 --> 00:14:49,962
And if you expand that over a 20-year life, the 3% improvement in fuel efficiency correlates to about 200,000 gallons less diesel fuel is burned over the life of the

199
00:14:49,963 --> 00:14:50,663
product.

200
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:57,220
With the frame now upright, the engine and other components are ready to be put in place.

201
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:02,820
This is really where the locomotive assembly process turns into an automated assembly line.

202
00:15:02,821 --> 00:15:08,460
The platform from station one is brought down and put into its upright position.

203
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:13,580
And here we will start to place the individual cabs and components on the platform

204
00:15:13,581 --> 00:15:17,020
and start to do the final assembly of the locomotive.

205
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:23,400
The locomotive starts taking shape as it advances down the line

206
00:15:23,401 --> 00:15:27,100
in what workers call the world's largest air hockey table.

207
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:31,200
What happens is we'll connect an air hose to the air jacks.

208
00:15:31,201 --> 00:15:35,020
the air will slightly elevate the locomotive platform

209
00:15:35,021 --> 00:15:38,560
and move it down from one workstation to the other,

210
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,920
which is kind of impressive when you think of,

211
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:45,420
as it nears the final workstation, it's over 300,000 pounds.

212
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:52,080
But the 75-foot-long, 16-foot-high steel behemoths aren't finished yet.

213
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:55,340
At this point, the locomotive is essentially complete,

214
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:56,780
except for the truck assemblies.

215
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:00,360
From here, we'll lift the locomotive assembly off the air jacks

216
00:16:00,361 --> 00:16:03,600
and take it over to Station 4.

217
00:16:03,601 --> 00:16:06,820
A crane operator lifts the 150-ton locomotive

218
00:16:06,821 --> 00:16:11,160
over the factory floor and deposits it gently

219
00:16:11,161 --> 00:16:13,640
onto a set of trucks or wheel assemblies.

220
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:22,000
After the locomotive is completed in Station 4,

221
00:16:22,001 --> 00:16:24,480
it's brought back to one of our paint booths.

222
00:16:24,481 --> 00:16:26,960
And you can see here, it's in the initial stages

223
00:16:26,961 --> 00:16:30,260
of being prepared for the final paint job.

224
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:33,560
Specific colors and logos to identify a company's freight

225
00:16:33,561 --> 00:16:36,400
is key for safety and brand recognition.

226
00:16:36,401 --> 00:16:39,220
And with that, a custom paint job is complete

227
00:16:39,221 --> 00:16:41,060
for this BNSF locomotive.

228
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:46,340
In the initial years of the railroad,

229
00:16:46,341 --> 00:16:48,760
the biggest obstacle was simply making sure

230
00:16:48,761 --> 00:16:51,800
the trains carrying expensive cargo could go the distance.

231
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:56,620
This shiny new Goliath is much smarter and much cleaner

232
00:16:56,621 --> 00:16:58,640
than the power and might that ruled the rails

233
00:16:58,641 --> 00:17:00,600
for nearly two centuries before.

234
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:05,740
The development of the steam locomotive

235
00:17:05,741 --> 00:17:08,880
was both evolutionary and revolutionary.

236
00:17:08,881 --> 00:17:11,880
The biggest problem initially was finding the proportions,

237
00:17:11,881 --> 00:17:15,160
the proportions between the boiler, the cylinders,

238
00:17:15,161 --> 00:17:18,060
the drivers, and the linkage mechanisms.

239
00:17:18,061 --> 00:17:21,000
The first locomotive that really got it all right

240
00:17:21,001 --> 00:17:23,240
in the right proportions was a locomotive

241
00:17:23,241 --> 00:17:24,500
called the John Bull.

242
00:17:26,360 --> 00:17:28,500
Mechanics put the John Bull into commission

243
00:17:28,501 --> 00:17:32,580
in 1831, and it became the first locomotive ever

244
00:17:32,581 --> 00:17:34,440
to be duplicated.

245
00:17:34,441 --> 00:17:37,520
But the wood-burning model was quickly outmoded,

246
00:17:37,521 --> 00:17:42,440
as locomotives increased rapidly in size and power.

247
00:17:42,441 --> 00:17:45,280
Wood was used generally in the American railroads

248
00:17:45,281 --> 00:17:47,700
till sometime right after the Civil War.

249
00:17:47,701 --> 00:17:50,740
And what happened was that a lot of territories

250
00:17:50,741 --> 00:17:53,300
that the railroads are going in, in the prairies and the plains,

251
00:17:53,301 --> 00:17:55,960
there weren't any trees around.

252
00:17:55,961 --> 00:17:59,560
Most locomotives introduced at the end of the 19th century burned coal.

253
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:06,820
Back in those days, about every three, four years, you'd get a larger locomotive.

254
00:18:07,020 --> 00:18:10,640
They just kept advancing the technology every couple of years.

255
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:12,500
And it's like your automobile.

256
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:17,940
Somebody would come out with a new model, and the railroads would try it out and find out that,

257
00:18:17,941 --> 00:18:21,100
yeah, this is pretty efficient, and we'll buy some of these.

258
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:25,300
Early 20th century locomotives easily dwarfed their predecessors.

259
00:18:25,301 --> 00:18:28,800
Their size and power may have changed drastically.

260
00:18:28,801 --> 00:18:31,800
However, there were a few fundamental changes

261
00:18:31,801 --> 00:18:32,800
in how they worked.

262
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:38,800
Pressurized steam enters one side of the cylinder

263
00:18:38,801 --> 00:18:40,800
and pushes the piston away.

264
00:18:40,801 --> 00:18:43,800
A valve shifts the steam to the other side

265
00:18:43,801 --> 00:18:45,800
and forces the piston back.

266
00:18:45,801 --> 00:18:49,300
A series of connected rods convert the piston's back-and-forth

267
00:18:49,301 --> 00:18:53,800
motion to the rotational motion of the wheels.

268
00:18:53,801 --> 00:19:00,800
By the Second World War, the steam locomotive had reached its pinnacle of technological development,

269
00:19:00,801 --> 00:19:03,800
and it had an unlikely replacement.

270
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:09,800
The diesel was introduced in the 1920s.

271
00:19:09,801 --> 00:19:14,800
It was not considered to be a real road-worthy locomotive.

272
00:19:14,801 --> 00:19:19,800
They were used as switch engines, they were used in specialties, they were used in lightweight streamliners,

273
00:19:19,801 --> 00:19:22,800
but they weren't really considered a threat.

274
00:19:23,800 --> 00:19:33,198
The Electromotive Corporation in Cleveland, Ohio, decided that until they could replace a full-size steam locomotive with a diesel, the diesel wasn't going to get

275
00:19:33,199 --> 00:19:33,899
anywhere.

276
00:19:34,220 --> 00:19:46,280
So in 1939, they created the FT freight diesel, which was a two-unit diesel, had two 16-cylinder engines rated at 1,350 horsepower each.

277
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:50,420
And that locomotive single-handedly killed the steam engine.

278
00:19:50,421 --> 00:19:56,580
For railroaders like Bernie O'Brien, who spent decades working on steam locomotives

279
00:19:56,581 --> 00:20:00,620
like the Union Pacific Challenger, the switch to diesel was bittersweet.

280
00:20:00,621 --> 00:20:04,900
It was a sad thing for me to see that happen.

281
00:20:04,901 --> 00:20:10,360
Our last Challengers were only six years old when they phased them out of service, which

282
00:20:10,361 --> 00:20:13,600
is just pretty hard to believe.

283
00:20:13,601 --> 00:20:19,060
The steam locomotive did not fail as a machine because it wasn't a good puller of freight.

284
00:20:19,061 --> 00:20:22,520
The steam locomotive was extremely labor intensive,

285
00:20:22,521 --> 00:20:25,840
and it required a lot of care.

286
00:20:25,841 --> 00:20:27,640
The diesel is like your automobile.

287
00:20:27,641 --> 00:20:28,720
It's automatic.

288
00:20:28,721 --> 00:20:30,920
You fire it up and it goes.

289
00:20:30,921 --> 00:20:32,920
The mechanics of a diesel locomotive

290
00:20:32,921 --> 00:20:37,480
are very different from those of a steam engine.

291
00:20:37,481 --> 00:20:39,760
In the cylinder, a fuel-air mixture

292
00:20:39,761 --> 00:20:43,280
is compressed by the piston to a point where it ignites.

293
00:20:43,281 --> 00:20:45,800
The explosion forces the piston down,

294
00:20:45,801 --> 00:20:48,860
which turns the crankshaft.

295
00:20:48,861 --> 00:20:53,000
An alternator then converts the motion of the crankshaft into electrical power,

296
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:59,100
which is fed down to individual traction motors on each axle to turn the wheels of a locomotive.

297
00:21:01,280 --> 00:21:07,260
Well, one of the advantages of diesel power is that you could couple as many units together

298
00:21:07,261 --> 00:21:10,520
as you needed for power to get it over the road.

299
00:21:10,521 --> 00:21:16,380
It just depends upon how much power you needed, and one man controlled all of those units.

300
00:21:16,381 --> 00:21:18,980
Like their steam predecessors,

301
00:21:19,080 --> 00:21:21,920
each generation of diesel electric locomotives

302
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:24,520
continued to outpace the last.

303
00:21:24,620 --> 00:21:26,920
Today's freight locomotives are able to haul

304
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:30,020
more tonnage than ever, but it's a feat that requires

305
00:21:30,120 --> 00:21:31,520
more than just horsepower.

306
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:38,340
With its punishing uphill grade, tight curves,

307
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:42,280
and steep descent, Southern California's Cajon Pass

308
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:44,680
is one of the most treacherous railroad routes

309
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:47,680
in the United States.

310
00:21:47,681 --> 00:21:50,180
Patrick Marquez, a locomotive engineer

311
00:21:50,181 --> 00:21:52,680
for the Burlington Northern Santa Fe,

312
00:21:52,681 --> 00:21:54,180
used to make the grinding journey

313
00:21:54,181 --> 00:21:57,680
at the mountain pass several times a week.

314
00:21:57,681 --> 00:22:00,180
He does this with a mile and a half of freight,

315
00:22:00,181 --> 00:22:06,680
which weighs upwards of 6,000 tons trailing behind the engines.

316
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:08,680
It's normally a two-man operation.

317
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:11,180
A conductor who serves as the manager of the train

318
00:22:11,181 --> 00:22:13,680
and its rolling stock...

319
00:22:13,681 --> 00:22:17,960
BNSF 7644 East Farmer Bryant, over.

320
00:22:17,961 --> 00:22:21,720
And the engineer, or train driver.

321
00:22:21,721 --> 00:22:24,460
But for this grueling journey, a second engineer

322
00:22:24,461 --> 00:22:26,820
with two helper locomotives couples

323
00:22:26,821 --> 00:22:30,460
onto the rear of the train to push it to the top of the hill.

324
00:22:34,700 --> 00:22:36,260
Helper's going to contact me and let

325
00:22:36,261 --> 00:22:37,840
me know when he's getting a release

326
00:22:37,841 --> 00:22:40,080
on the rear end of the train, and then we'll leave.

327
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:46,020
Here we go.

328
00:22:46,021 --> 00:22:49,020
So now he just gave me the signal that air brakes are releasing.

329
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:54,060
We come right off the brakes, give some throttle,

330
00:22:54,061 --> 00:22:54,800
and we take off.

331
00:22:58,300 --> 00:22:59,600
As soon as we take this corner, you're

332
00:22:59,601 --> 00:23:02,160
going to notice our speed dropping dramatically,

333
00:23:02,161 --> 00:23:04,800
because the hill is all the way up that to the summit.

334
00:23:07,240 --> 00:23:10,940
The engines must provide 64,000 pounds of tractive effort

335
00:23:10,941 --> 00:23:12,240
to propel the train forward.

336
00:23:13,680 --> 00:23:18,420
The more power we exert into the traction motor,

337
00:23:18,421 --> 00:23:22,680
the more likely that that wheel has the opportunity to spin.

338
00:23:22,681 --> 00:23:26,000
In order to keep it from spinning, we'll use sand,

339
00:23:26,001 --> 00:23:31,260
and that will put a grit or sandpaper underneath the wheel

340
00:23:31,261 --> 00:23:35,840
so we don't have metal to metal, and it'll keep it moving.

341
00:23:35,841 --> 00:23:38,740
Today's locomotives are equipped with an electronic traction

342
00:23:38,741 --> 00:23:40,320
control system.

343
00:23:40,321 --> 00:23:43,060
The system uses compressed air to spray sand

344
00:23:43,061 --> 00:23:45,700
from a nozzle in front of each wheel.

345
00:23:45,701 --> 00:23:48,800
If I spin my wheels, the computer will sense a wheel

346
00:23:48,801 --> 00:23:50,860
slip, like on a car.

347
00:23:50,861 --> 00:23:51,860
You know, you feel it.

348
00:23:51,861 --> 00:23:53,860
And you'll also feel it in here.

349
00:23:53,861 --> 00:23:55,520
And I'll get a little signal in here.

350
00:23:55,521 --> 00:23:57,600
It'll say, wheel slip.

351
00:23:57,601 --> 00:23:59,700
Before the helper is clear block, I'll plug back.

352
00:23:59,701 --> 00:24:02,260
Engineer Marquez is in constant communication

353
00:24:02,261 --> 00:24:04,160
with the helper locomotive engineer

354
00:24:04,161 --> 00:24:06,000
at the back of his train.

355
00:24:06,001 --> 00:24:07,500
If we didn't pick up those helpers,

356
00:24:07,501 --> 00:24:09,620
we'd probably be getting a lot of wheel slip.

357
00:24:09,621 --> 00:24:11,720
The wheels would just be slipping.

358
00:24:11,721 --> 00:24:15,320
we wouldn't have enough power to pull this up the hill.

359
00:24:15,321 --> 00:24:18,300
It takes a lot of effort to get more than 12 million pounds

360
00:24:18,301 --> 00:24:20,300
to the top of the hill.

361
00:24:20,301 --> 00:24:22,260
Gravity's pulling it that way.

362
00:24:22,261 --> 00:24:23,660
I'm trying to pull it that way, right?

363
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:35,140
If we stopped out here, and if I released my brakes

364
00:24:35,141 --> 00:24:39,140
without giving it power, we'd start rolling back.

365
00:24:39,141 --> 00:24:40,880
With all the tools on board to keep

366
00:24:40,881 --> 00:24:45,855
his train moving forward, Marquez can sit back and enjoy the ride to the top.

367
00:24:45,856 --> 00:24:46,760
Well, sort of.

368
00:24:46,761 --> 00:24:53,891
That's an alerter. It's like a safety device. Just basically to keep me awake.

369
00:24:53,892 --> 00:24:54,592
If I'm

370
00:24:54,593 --> 00:25:00,572
capacitated somehow and the alerter times out, it'll apply the brakes at a service rate.

371
00:25:00,573 --> 00:25:01,273
It'll

372
00:25:01,274 --> 00:25:03,744
just slow the train down gradually and just come to a stop.

373
00:25:03,745 --> 00:25:05,420
I just hit this button, the alerter

374
00:25:05,421 --> 00:25:07,920
reset that every time pops up.

375
00:25:07,921 --> 00:25:10,820
If I do something with the throttle, brakes,

376
00:25:10,821 --> 00:25:12,760
something like that, it'll reset it too,

377
00:25:12,761 --> 00:25:14,980
because it's showing that I'm awake, you know.

378
00:25:14,981 --> 00:25:15,980
I'll move the controls.

379
00:25:18,660 --> 00:25:20,960
Marquez has to stop his train at the summit

380
00:25:20,961 --> 00:25:23,920
to let off the helper engines.

381
00:25:23,921 --> 00:25:26,320
All I got to do is throttle down,

382
00:25:26,321 --> 00:25:28,480
and the train will stall out, come to a stop.

383
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:35,040
This stop was planned, but not all of them

384
00:25:35,041 --> 00:25:38,800
can be.

385
00:25:38,801 --> 00:25:40,920
If we're moving 20 mile an hour and we're coming down

386
00:25:40,921 --> 00:25:44,880
to Mountain Gray territory, and this train weighs roughly

387
00:25:44,881 --> 00:25:48,160
6,000 ton or more, it could possibly take us a quarter

388
00:25:48,161 --> 00:25:53,940
of a mile to stop the train in an emergency application.

389
00:25:53,941 --> 00:25:56,280
And as every early railroader knew,

390
00:25:56,281 --> 00:25:58,500
being unable to stop a speeding train

391
00:25:58,501 --> 00:26:01,080
was, well, a train wreck.

392
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:10,200
The earliest railroads used hand brakes of various types.

393
00:26:10,480 --> 00:26:15,060
Often this was as simple as a lever with a wooden block going against the wheel.

394
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:20,440
As the cars got bigger, they would go to what we would call a stem winder

395
00:26:20,441 --> 00:26:24,960
that tightened a chain that pulled iron shoes against the wheels,

396
00:26:24,961 --> 00:26:27,600
but they all had to be done by hand.

397
00:26:29,140 --> 00:26:33,880
Every five or six cars would have a brake man who had to apply the brakes on each of his cars.

398
00:26:33,881 --> 00:26:42,675
And the brakeman was probably one of the most dangerous jobs in railroading because there was this chance that you would be thrown off the car or lose your footing in

399
00:26:42,676 --> 00:26:43,376
that.

400
00:26:43,377 --> 00:26:49,600
But you had to walk from car to car on the roofs of the cars applying the handbrakes in order to stop the train.

401
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:54,740
But back then, the workers were considered expendable, and that's what it took to stop trains.

402
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:03,120
In 1866, George Westinghouse took on the challenge of finding a more effective way to stop a train.

403
00:27:03,121 --> 00:27:07,120
And by 1873, he developed what remains to this day

404
00:27:07,121 --> 00:27:11,120
one of the most significant safety inventions of all time,

405
00:27:11,121 --> 00:27:14,120
the automatic air brake.

406
00:27:14,121 --> 00:27:18,120
The air brakes on a freight train are supplied with compressed air from the locomotive.

407
00:27:18,121 --> 00:27:24,120
As the air reservoir in each car fills, it trips a valve to release the brakes on that car.

408
00:27:26,120 --> 00:27:31,120
Only when the last car disengages its brakes can the train move.

409
00:27:31,121 --> 00:27:35,720
The system is failsafe, as any reduction of this air pressure,

410
00:27:35,721 --> 00:27:40,120
such as a rupture in the air supply hose, causes the brakes to be applied.

411
00:27:40,121 --> 00:27:44,560
When Diesel Electric replaced the steam locomotive,

412
00:27:44,561 --> 00:27:47,520
it brought with it another revolutionary technology,

413
00:27:47,521 --> 00:27:50,620
dynamic braking to supplement air brakes.

414
00:27:50,621 --> 00:27:53,620
Back at the Cajon Pass,

415
00:27:53,621 --> 00:27:57,460
engineer Marquez relies upon dynamic braking

416
00:27:57,461 --> 00:28:01,120
to start his downhill descent. I'm in dynamic braking now,

417
00:28:01,121 --> 00:28:03,960
it just turns the traction motors into big resistors.

418
00:28:07,100 --> 00:28:09,620
Instead of feeding power to the wheels,

419
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:13,700
the traction motors now take power from the wheels.

420
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:16,240
The energy is dissipated as heat.

421
00:28:16,241 --> 00:28:20,640
The underpowered traction motors have become hard to turn,

422
00:28:20,641 --> 00:28:24,480
thereby providing resistance to the wheels, slowing the train.

423
00:28:27,740 --> 00:28:29,620
The more I move this lever forward,

424
00:28:29,621 --> 00:28:33,460
It demands more amps, which basically slows the motors down,

425
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:34,900
adds resistance to them.

426
00:28:34,980 --> 00:28:36,880
So they're the ones slowing us down now,

427
00:28:37,160 --> 00:28:39,580
which means I've got to be prepared to add more amps

428
00:28:39,581 --> 00:28:41,020
to keep my train under control.

429
00:28:43,660 --> 00:28:46,080
Dynamic brakes are very useful on downgrades

430
00:28:46,081 --> 00:28:49,340
and save a considerable amount of wear on air brake shoes.

431
00:28:53,020 --> 00:28:54,900
Gravity's just pulling us downhill.

432
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:58,680
Like I said, I have resistance on my head end

433
00:28:58,681 --> 00:29:03,280
because I turn these traction motors into resistors.

434
00:29:03,281 --> 00:29:05,280
So the wheels are turning around, trying to slow us down.

435
00:29:05,281 --> 00:29:07,060
The engines are trying to slow us down.

436
00:29:07,061 --> 00:29:09,400
The train's just pushing against our engine,

437
00:29:09,401 --> 00:29:12,560
and I can try and control my speed with the dynamic brakes.

438
00:29:12,561 --> 00:29:14,900
As we get faster, if I don't want to go too fast,

439
00:29:14,901 --> 00:29:18,040
I'll add more dynamic brakes trying to slow down

440
00:29:18,041 --> 00:29:20,960
or keep our speed constant.

441
00:29:20,961 --> 00:29:25,740
If I want to gain some speed, I can reduce my dynamic braking,

442
00:29:25,741 --> 00:29:28,240
which means the wheels will turn easier on the engines.

443
00:29:28,241 --> 00:29:29,660
It means gravity will shove more.

444
00:29:31,580 --> 00:29:36,640
But the dynamic brakes may not be enough to keep this freight train from becoming a roller coaster ride.

445
00:29:37,460 --> 00:29:39,280
I'm in full dynamic brakes.

446
00:29:39,720 --> 00:29:43,980
I'm demanding 35 kilopounds from my motors and all that resistance.

447
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:45,940
The train's just going too fast.

448
00:29:46,520 --> 00:29:48,940
I'm just going to keep picking up speed unless I do something.

449
00:29:49,880 --> 00:29:52,340
So now I'll slow my train down with some air brakes.

450
00:29:54,480 --> 00:29:57,860
Essentially, he's riding the brakes the rest of the journey down.

451
00:29:58,240 --> 00:30:04,640
When Marquez's 6,000-ton train reaches the bottom,

452
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:10,240
it becomes yet another train that has successfully negotiated the Cajon Pass

453
00:30:10,241 --> 00:30:15,400
and transported hundreds of freight containers over the mountains.

454
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:20,840
And the containers it carries are significant beyond the goods they hold.

455
00:30:21,700 --> 00:30:23,860
With the increase in imports coming from Asia,

456
00:30:24,500 --> 00:30:28,180
these freight containers have come to rejuvenate the railroad industry.

457
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:36,080
Over 7 million containers enter the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach every year.

458
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:40,920
More than anywhere else in the United States.

459
00:30:41,920 --> 00:30:47,180
They are intermodal loads, containers designed to flow easily from one mode of transport to another.

460
00:30:47,740 --> 00:30:51,060
And they have revolutionized and revitalized the rail industry.

461
00:30:51,740 --> 00:30:56,900
Intermodal containers are an extension of the container ship operations that are here at the Port of Los Angeles.

462
00:30:56,901 --> 00:30:59,660
Containers come off the ship, they move on to the dock,

463
00:30:59,661 --> 00:31:01,440
and then they're either moved out by truck

464
00:31:01,441 --> 00:31:04,280
or moved out by rail.

465
00:31:04,281 --> 00:31:07,140
Over 50 trains leave the twin ports of Los Angeles

466
00:31:07,141 --> 00:31:09,820
and Long Beach on a daily basis,

467
00:31:09,821 --> 00:31:14,520
each carrying approximately 220 containers.

468
00:31:14,521 --> 00:31:17,860
That keeps about 11,000 trucks off congested Los Angeles

469
00:31:17,861 --> 00:31:21,700
freeways every day.

470
00:31:21,701 --> 00:31:25,420
The advent of trade with Asia, especially in the last 20 years,

471
00:31:25,421 --> 00:31:27,180
and specifically China in the last 10,

472
00:31:27,181 --> 00:31:30,860
has doubled the amount of volume that is coming into the West Coast ports

473
00:31:30,861 --> 00:31:32,420
over that period of time.

474
00:31:32,421 --> 00:31:37,420
And the container trains have really been a way to be an outlet for that for large volumes.

475
00:31:39,020 --> 00:31:41,700
And intermodal train traffic coming out of the ports

476
00:31:41,701 --> 00:31:46,420
is expected to double to 100 trains daily in the next two decades.

477
00:31:46,421 --> 00:31:51,420
Over 60% of the cargo that comes through these ports, containerized cargo,

478
00:31:51,421 --> 00:31:54,740
in the form of imports, is destined for the rest of the nation.

479
00:31:54,741 --> 00:31:57,400
And the first stop, or the first passage, if you will,

480
00:31:57,401 --> 00:31:59,520
for that traffic is on the Alameda Corridors.

481
00:31:59,521 --> 00:32:03,220
It heads east to service the rest of the nation.

482
00:32:03,221 --> 00:32:05,420
The 20-mile-long corridor is essentially

483
00:32:05,421 --> 00:32:09,860
an expressway for trains,

484
00:32:09,861 --> 00:32:13,060
connecting the ports to rail yards east of the city.

485
00:32:13,061 --> 00:32:17,560
This 10-mile section that we fondly refer to as the trench

486
00:32:17,561 --> 00:32:19,440
was kind of the crown jewel of the program.

487
00:32:19,441 --> 00:32:22,240
It's 10 miles long, represents about one-half

488
00:32:22,241 --> 00:32:24,740
the length of the overall corridor.

489
00:32:24,741 --> 00:32:28,440
In the trench, train travels unimpeded beneath city traffic.

490
00:32:29,320 --> 00:32:34,040
A journey that once took almost two hours has been cut to only 45 minutes.

491
00:32:34,540 --> 00:32:36,140
It's really a national asset.

492
00:32:37,060 --> 00:32:41,260
What the Alameda Corridor represents is probably one of the strongest links

493
00:32:41,261 --> 00:32:44,060
in the transcontinental rail network at this point in time.

494
00:32:47,340 --> 00:32:51,980
Intermodal freight has surpassed coal as the number one revenue generator for the rail industry.

495
00:32:51,981 --> 00:32:57,460
And the articulated well car, designed to carry intermodal containers,

496
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:01,920
helped railroads remain relevant in the competitive world of freight transportation.

497
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:07,080
Each car is able to fit one container in a depressed well area.

498
00:33:07,900 --> 00:33:12,120
With another resting on top, the well area lowers the height of the load,

499
00:33:12,640 --> 00:33:17,180
allowing it to make bridge and tunnel clearances, as well as lowering its center of gravity.

500
00:33:17,820 --> 00:33:22,460
The double-stack opportunity provides two for the price of one in a given car space.

501
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:26,100
So the loading volumes can increase, the cost per unit drops,

502
00:33:26,200 --> 00:33:30,380
and makes the rail a very, very efficient and cost-effective way to move cargo.

503
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:34,180
Of course, the well car isn't the first freight car to fill a special need.

504
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:39,880
The earliest freight cars were simple platforms with wheels attached.

505
00:33:40,340 --> 00:33:42,540
But soon enough, sides were added.

506
00:33:43,320 --> 00:33:46,140
This particular type of car here is a gondola.

507
00:33:46,141 --> 00:33:48,940
Now, it's gondola, not gondola.

508
00:33:48,941 --> 00:33:50,140
All those are in Venice.

509
00:33:50,141 --> 00:33:52,140
There are none on America's railroads.

510
00:33:52,141 --> 00:33:56,140
This particular type of car was one of the earlier types of cars.

511
00:33:56,141 --> 00:34:00,140
But gondolas were no match for harsh winters,

512
00:34:00,141 --> 00:34:05,140
extremely hot summers, and long treks between settled areas of the U.S.

513
00:34:05,141 --> 00:34:08,140
This called for an enclosed car.

514
00:34:08,141 --> 00:34:13,140
Thus, the boxcar, an icon of American freight railroading, was born.

515
00:34:13,141 --> 00:34:18,140
Boxcars often functioned as rolling billboards for the railroads they served.

516
00:34:18,141 --> 00:34:21,140
Boxcars were modified in several other car types.

517
00:34:21,141 --> 00:34:26,140
With open slats on the side, it was modified into a cattle car to carry cattle.

518
00:34:26,141 --> 00:34:33,140
With heavy insulation and ice bunkers in either end, it could be used as a refrigerator car to haul perishable items.

519
00:34:33,141 --> 00:34:38,140
Refers, or refrigerator cars, changed the way America ate.

520
00:34:38,141 --> 00:34:43,820
The refrigerator car was essentially an ice box on wheels, and this worked well until

521
00:34:43,821 --> 00:34:47,600
the advent of the frozen food industry.

522
00:34:47,601 --> 00:34:49,520
Enter the mechanical refrigerator car.

523
00:34:49,521 --> 00:34:55,980
In the early 1950s, a small diesel engine was used to power a refrigeration device.

524
00:34:55,981 --> 00:35:01,340
The cars then became, instead of ice boxes on wheels, actual refrigerators on wheels.

525
00:35:01,341 --> 00:35:05,500
This spelled the doom of the iced reefer.

526
00:35:05,501 --> 00:35:11,220
The rail industry was able to accommodate just about every market in the United States.

527
00:35:11,221 --> 00:35:14,740
The petroleum age led to the introduction of the tank car.

528
00:35:14,741 --> 00:35:19,360
The automobile revolution led to the emergence of the auto rack.

529
00:35:19,361 --> 00:35:25,160
And the piggyback, or TOFC trailer on flat car, was developed in response to trucking

530
00:35:25,161 --> 00:35:27,180
competition in the 1950s.

531
00:35:27,181 --> 00:35:32,620
But with the proliferation of freight car designs came the demise of another railroad

532
00:35:32,621 --> 00:35:35,260
car, the caboose.

533
00:35:35,261 --> 00:35:38,900
Radios made possible to work with fewer people.

534
00:35:39,240 --> 00:35:42,520
So you didn't need four or five people to run a train.

535
00:35:42,620 --> 00:35:45,860
You could run a train pretty effectively with two or three people.

536
00:35:46,160 --> 00:35:50,540
The caboose became an unnecessary appendage on the rear of the train.

537
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:54,420
Technology caught up with the caboose in the form of FRED.

538
00:35:55,280 --> 00:35:57,540
FRED means flashing rear-end device.

539
00:35:58,140 --> 00:36:03,020
For the men who lost their jobs as a result of this, the F had a different connotation.

540
00:36:05,260 --> 00:36:12,260
The dumb Fred can be anything from a blinking light to a simple red flag stuck on the end of the last car.

541
00:36:12,261 --> 00:36:20,260
Smart Fred, or the end of train device as it came to be called, can radio information about the airline pressure

542
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:25,260
as well as let the engineer know what the cars at the end of the train are doing.

543
00:36:25,261 --> 00:36:29,260
But it's not just the end of the train that's gotten smarter.

544
00:36:29,261 --> 00:36:32,800
Railroad operations are moving full throttle

545
00:36:32,801 --> 00:36:36,960
to the very cutting edge of the information age.

546
00:36:40,900 --> 00:36:44,040
At any given time, there are thousands of trains

547
00:36:44,041 --> 00:36:46,460
snaking their way across the North American continent.

548
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:49,600
Their paths intersect.

549
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:51,460
They share some of the same rail

550
00:36:51,461 --> 00:36:54,840
and may even use the same line in opposing directions.

551
00:36:55,840 --> 00:36:58,340
It may sound like a train wreck just waiting to happen.

552
00:36:59,260 --> 00:37:09,900
But every move of every train is closely monitored and calculated by dispatchers located at operation

553
00:37:09,901 --> 00:37:11,640
centers throughout the United States.

554
00:37:12,300 --> 00:37:17,260
The train crew has a responsibility to operate the train, but the train dispatcher gives them

555
00:37:17,261 --> 00:37:18,060
the route to run on.

556
00:37:19,240 --> 00:37:25,760
Train dispatchers are the rulers of the rails, and no move can be made without their authorization.

557
00:37:25,761 --> 00:37:34,080
BNSF Railway's Network Operations Center is command central for the dispatchers of one

558
00:37:34,081 --> 00:37:36,080
of the largest railroads in North America.

559
00:37:36,081 --> 00:37:41,700
The facility is 45,000 square feet, about the size of a football field.

560
00:37:41,701 --> 00:37:45,520
We house at any given time about 225 employees.

561
00:37:45,521 --> 00:37:51,400
There's 94 different dispatching workstations.

562
00:37:51,401 --> 00:37:57,720
dispatcher's territory is 200 to 500 miles and their planning horizon is two to three hours ahead

563
00:37:59,640 --> 00:38:04,200
he or she has a screen with a track layout on the computer screen and it's all point and click the

564
00:38:04,201 --> 00:38:10,760
dispatcher can point to a switch or a route and direct that train's movement over that

565
00:38:10,761 --> 00:38:16,920
which is just a point and click operation this way it's the dispatcher that selects the path of the

566
00:38:16,921 --> 00:38:20,360
the train, not the engineer.

567
00:38:20,361 --> 00:38:23,460
So it's imperative that the dispatchers know exactly where

568
00:38:23,461 --> 00:38:25,760
their trains are at all times.

569
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:27,600
We've got two different tracking mechanisms.

570
00:38:27,601 --> 00:38:30,440
One's through the signal system that actually tracks trains

571
00:38:30,441 --> 00:38:33,480
through electric current in the rail that brings the information

572
00:38:33,481 --> 00:38:35,700
into the train dispatcher's dispatching system.

573
00:38:35,701 --> 00:38:39,600
And then secondly, we've got a wireless AEI automatic equipment

574
00:38:39,601 --> 00:38:43,240
identification that wirelessly transmits information

575
00:38:43,241 --> 00:38:46,320
into the train dispatcher and gives him or her location

576
00:38:46,321 --> 00:38:47,021
route.

577
00:38:47,022 --> 00:38:53,580
And this is basically an RFID that we place on all the rail cars, all the locomotives,

578
00:38:53,581 --> 00:38:56,917
on every rail car in North America.

579
00:38:56,918 --> 00:39:02,160
BNSF has around 500 AEI readers and as the trains go by

580
00:39:02,161 --> 00:39:09,000
these readers it energizes the tag and captures what car that is or what locomotive that is and

581
00:39:09,001 --> 00:39:15,240
then we use that information to update our mainframe and identify exactly what's on the train at that

582
00:39:15,241 --> 00:39:17,480
point in time.

583
00:39:17,481 --> 00:39:20,740
Within a few seconds of a train going by a reader,

584
00:39:20,741 --> 00:39:23,740
the information is available to the dispatchers at the network

585
00:39:23,741 --> 00:39:28,380
operations center, as well as to customers online.

586
00:39:28,381 --> 00:39:31,480
But since readers may be up to 100 miles apart,

587
00:39:31,481 --> 00:39:35,220
and conductors set out cars at various points along the route,

588
00:39:35,221 --> 00:39:38,860
dispatchers need even more accurate reporting.

589
00:39:38,861 --> 00:39:45,240
BNSF on a daily basis has around 1,400 trains in operation.

590
00:39:45,241 --> 00:39:46,580
The trains go through a reader,

591
00:39:47,280 --> 00:39:48,600
reporting critical information

592
00:39:48,601 --> 00:39:50,580
through the Voice Train Reporting application.

593
00:39:51,640 --> 00:39:54,500
The application automatically converts verbal information

594
00:39:54,501 --> 00:39:56,200
into computer data.

595
00:39:56,660 --> 00:39:59,120
So they basically just use the radio that they have,

596
00:39:59,200 --> 00:40:00,300
they tell us what they did.

597
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:02,340
Within nine seconds,

598
00:40:02,540 --> 00:40:04,240
that information's updated in our mainframe.

599
00:40:05,360 --> 00:40:07,240
This information can be anything

600
00:40:07,241 --> 00:40:09,560
from picking up cars to leaving cars out

601
00:40:09,561 --> 00:40:11,960
at various sidetracks and yards along the route.

602
00:40:11,961 --> 00:40:17,160
Handheld computers are also used to report car movement.

603
00:40:18,360 --> 00:40:23,400
This would be very similar to what UPS does in that that's how they keep track of their packages.

604
00:40:24,200 --> 00:40:26,120
This is how we keep track of our rail cars.

605
00:40:26,220 --> 00:40:29,080
And if you consider a rail car as just a lot bigger package,

606
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:35,560
this is basically where we track where we left the car, when we left it there,

607
00:40:35,561 --> 00:40:40,360
and it's how we identify to the conductor you need to go pick up that package.

608
00:40:40,361 --> 00:40:47,360
Railroads are now exploring innovative ways to utilize Wi-Fi and GPS technologies to increase rail safety.

609
00:40:47,361 --> 00:40:53,360
An example of a safety initiative is electronic train management system, or ETMS as we call it.

610
00:41:02,360 --> 00:41:09,360
The locomotive on-board system would have the authority that's granted to the train, the speed, the distance,

611
00:41:09,361 --> 00:41:13,180
the distance that they have to travel with the authority that's been issued.

612
00:41:13,620 --> 00:41:16,440
The system automatically takes control of the train

613
00:41:16,441 --> 00:41:20,200
if the engineer exceeds the authority given to him by the dispatcher,

614
00:41:20,840 --> 00:41:22,200
fails to acknowledge his signal,

615
00:41:22,800 --> 00:41:25,660
or even if he's about to take a curve too quickly.

616
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:30,500
The system constantly monitors information about the path ahead of the train

617
00:41:30,501 --> 00:41:33,440
and determines what actions are required from the engineer.

618
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:37,400
It gives a warning to the engineer if he's not taking the appropriate action

619
00:41:37,401 --> 00:41:39,500
to stop or slow down the train.

620
00:41:39,960 --> 00:41:43,960
If they violate that or don't react to stop the train

621
00:41:43,961 --> 00:41:45,400
before they get to the end of their authority,

622
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:46,960
it'll automatically stop the train.

623
00:41:48,560 --> 00:41:51,920
Once implemented, ETMS will allow railroads

624
00:41:51,921 --> 00:41:53,960
to run trains more safely and efficiently.

625
00:41:55,660 --> 00:41:59,060
So as we move forward faster than ever before,

626
00:41:59,420 --> 00:42:02,100
we can be sure that the rail industry will continue

627
00:42:02,101 --> 00:42:05,720
to be the best and most cost-effective way to move freight.

